## Decoding the question
Three limbs. "**Key features of the fourth wave**" — a definition, a date (about **2012** onwards), the global campaigns and four or five features. "**How have digital activism, #MeToo and the Aurat March reshaped … Pakistan**" — treat the three named items separately, then their **effects**. "**What backlash**" — the forms of backlash and why bodily autonomy provokes it. End with a **judgement**.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
The **fourth wave of feminism** (from about ==2012==) is feminist activism that uses **social media** to share experience, expose **harassment and violence**, organise quickly and hold the powerful to account. **Kira Cochrane** described it in *All the Rebel Women* (==2013==).

#### ii) The argument
In Pakistan the fourth wave arrived through **digital activism**, **#MeToo** (==2018==) and the **Aurat March** (from ==8 March 2018==). It brought new actors (young, urban women, workers and *khawaja siras*), new demands (**bodily autonomy**, safety, consent) and new methods (hashtags, marches, art). It won visibility and faster laws, but provoked a **fierce backlash** and remains limited by the **digital divide**.

### 2. Where the fourth wave came from

#### i) The early campaigns
The wave grew from a series of campaigns: **SlutWalk** (Toronto, ==2011==), protesting the idea that women's dress invites rape; **Laura Bates**'s **Everyday Sexism Project** (==2012==); and the protests after the **Delhi gang rape** of December ==2012==, which led to India's Criminal Law (Amendment) Act ==2013==.

#### ii) The global spread
**#NiUnaMenos** in Argentina (==2015==) and the Women's March of ==21 January 2017== carried the wave across continents.

#### iii) The defining moment: #MeToo
The phrase was first used by **Tarana Burke** in ==2006== and went global in **October 2017** after the reports on Harvey Weinstein.

### 3. Feature 1: social media as the organising tool

#### i) A phone, not an organisation
Earlier waves needed organisations, newspapers and conferences. The fourth wave needs a **phone**. Hashtags, testimony threads and viral videos let women share private experience instantly and mobilise across borders in hours.

#### ii) Lower barriers, global reach
This lowers the cost of joining, gives voice to women outside formal organisations and makes campaigns **global** — the same hashtag trends in Los Angeles, Buenos Aires and Lahore.

#### iii) Networked leadership
Leadership is looser too: campaigns are run by networks and collectives rather than by a single organisation or leader.

### 4. Feature 2: harassment, violence, consent and the body

#### i) The issues
The fourth wave focuses on **everyday sexism**, **sexual harassment**, **rape culture**, **consent** and **bodily autonomy** — the right to decide about one's own body, marriage and movement.

#### ii) The personal made public
It extends the second wave's "**the personal is political**" into public space and online space: street harassment, workplace abuse and online blackmail become political issues.

### 5. Feature 3: intersectionality and accountability

#### i) Intersectionality
Influenced by **Kimberlé Crenshaw**'s **intersectionality** (==1989==), the fourth wave insists that women's experience differs by class, race, religion, sexuality and disability, and includes trans people.

#### ii) Accountability
It also seeks **accountability** — "call-out culture" that names powerful abusers when institutions fail to act.

::: fig grid | Five features of the fourth wave.
@cols 3
Digital: hashtags, testimony, viral campaigns
Body: harassment, consent, bodily autonomy
Intersectional: class, race, religion, sexuality
Accountability: naming abusers; call-out culture
Global: the same campaign across many countries
:::

### 6. Digital activism in Pakistan

#### i) Early institutions
The **Digital Rights Foundation** (==2012==) opened a **cyber-harassment helpline** in ==2016==, the year the **Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act** (**PECA**) created offences of online harassment and blackmail.

#### ii) Outrage that forced action
Online outrage after the murder of **Qandeel Baloch** (==2016==), the **motorway gang rape** (==2020==) and the murder of **Noor Mukadam** (==2021==) forced the state to respond.

#### iii) More women online
The **mobile-internet gender gap** fell from **38%** to **25%** (GSMA, ==2024–25==), widening this space.

### 7. #MeToo in Pakistan

#### i) Breaking the silence
#MeToo reached Pakistan in ==2018==, with allegations of harassment in the entertainment industry and on campuses. It broke the silence around **workplace harassment**, encouraged women to use the **Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act 2010** (amended ==2022== to widen "workplace" and "employee"), and prompted universities and firms to form inquiry committees.

#### ii) The cost of speaking
Accusers faced **long defamation cases**, online abuse and social stigma — a reminder that speaking out is costly where institutions are weak.

### 8. The Aurat March

#### i) Origins
The **Aurat March**, first held in Karachi on ==8 March 2018== and then in Lahore, Islamabad and other cities, is Pakistan's fourth-wave movement.

#### ii) New actors
It is organised largely online by **young, urban women**, students and workers, with *khawaja siras*, home-based workers and domestic workers marching alongside.

#### iii) New demands
Violence and harassment, inheritance, forced and child marriage, workers' rights, *khawaja sira* rights, and **bodily autonomy** — symbolised by the slogan ***Mera Jism Meri Marzi*** ("my body, my choice").

#### iv) New language
Placards made for social media, humour, art and the language of **consent**, alongside the older language of the Constitution and Islam. The "**locus of resistance**" shifted from **legal rights** (WAF in the 1980s) to **bodily autonomy**.

### 9. Results

#### i) Visibility and faster laws
The fourth wave has made gender-based violence **visible** as never before and shortened the time between outrage and law: after the 2020 motorway case, the **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act** received assent on ==1 December 2021==.

#### ii) Inclusion
It has brought **new groups** — trans people, workers, students — into feminism, and pushed debates on domestic violence and child marriage that ended in the Islamabad laws of ==2025==.

#### iii) New voices and a new debate
It has changed **who speaks**: survivors now tell their own stories in public rather than through lawyers or NGOs, and young men increasingly appear as allies. Even critics of the march now discuss harassment, consent and online safety — a sign that the fourth wave has shifted the terms of public debate, much as the Delhi protests of ==2012== did in India.

### 10. The backlash

#### i) Forms of backlash

##### (a) In the media
Slogans on bodily autonomy were attacked on television and social media as **immoral** or "**Western**".

##### (b) In courts and streets
Petitions were filed in courts to stop the march; counter-events such as the "**Haya March**" were organised; posters were defaced.

##### (c) Online
Marchers faced **online abuse, threats and doxxing**.

#### ii) Why bodily autonomy provokes
The female body is tied to family ***izzat*** — critics hear a rejection of family and religion. Supporters reply that Islam forbids forced marriage and that **Article 14** guarantees dignity; on the substance — ending violence and forced marriage — there is wide agreement.

::: fig compare | The fourth wave in Pakistan: what it won and what it faced.
@vs vs
## Gains
- Visibility of violence and harassment
- Faster laws (Anti-Rape Act 2021)
- New actors: students, workers, trans people
## Backlash and limits
- "Western" and "immoral" charges
- Petitions, Haya March, online abuse
- Digital divide; urban, middle-class base
:::

### 11. Limits

#### i) The digital divide
Digital feminism reaches mainly **urban, educated** women; millions of rural women lack phones, data or the safety to speak online.

#### ii) Online weaknesses
Online campaigns can become "**slacktivism**" without organisation; call-outs raise **due-process** concerns; and polarised debate can alienate potential allies.

#### iii) The challenge
The movement must join the energy of the march with the patient **legal and grassroots work** of WAF, rural women's movements and NGOs.

### 12. Conclusion

#### i) What changed
The fourth wave — digital, body-centred and intersectional — has reshaped Pakistani feminism: it moved the struggle from **rights on paper to rights over one's own body**, brought new actors into the movement and made the state respond faster to violence.

#### ii) The future
It has also provoked the strongest backlash since the 1980s. Its future depends on reaching beyond the cities, framing its demands in the language of the **Constitution and Islam** as well as consent, and turning online outrage into **lasting institutions**.

## Why this answer scores
- Defines the fourth wave with **dates and campaigns** (2011–2017) and names **five features**.
- Treats the **three named items** — digital activism, #MeToo, the Aurat March — under separate headings.
- Explains **why** bodily autonomy provokes, and gives both sides of the backlash fairly.
- Uses current evidence: PECA 2016, the 2021 Anti-Rape Act, the GSMA's 38% to 25%.
- A **grid** and a **compare** figure organise the argument.
